{%- comment -%}
  Explainer figure: same accuracy, different deployments. Numbers from the
  Computer Networks paper's abstract (CESNET-TLS22, CNN embeddings vs Random
  Forest): macro F1 0.923 vs 0.925, about ten times faster training, seconds
  vs thousands of seconds for a new class, 171 MB vs 1,350 MB for 1.2 million
  flows, Random Forest faster per flow, unknown traffic at up to 0.92 AUROC.
{%- endcomment -%}
<figure class="note-figure fig-duel" style="--c: var(--line-traffic)">
  <p class="fig-title">Same accuracy, different deployments</p>
  <div class="duel" role="table" aria-label="Random Forest compared with classification by retrieval on six deployment criteria">
    <div class="duel-row duel-head" role="row">
      <span class="duel-crit" role="columnheader">Criterion</span>
      <span class="duel-a" role="columnheader">Random Forest</span>
      <span class="duel-b" role="columnheader">Retrieval</span>
    </div>
    <div class="duel-row" role="row">
      <span class="duel-crit" role="rowheader">Accuracy <small>macro F1</small></span>
      <span class="duel-a is-tie" role="cell">0.925</span>
      <span class="duel-b is-tie" role="cell">0.923</span>
    </div>
    <div class="duel-row" role="row">
      <span class="duel-crit" role="rowheader">Training</span>
      <span class="duel-a" role="cell">baseline</span>
      <span class="duel-b is-win" role="cell">about 10× faster</span>
    </div>
    <div class="duel-row" role="row">
      <span class="duel-crit" role="rowheader">Adding a new app</span>
      <span class="duel-a" role="cell">thousands of seconds</span>
      <span class="duel-b is-win" role="cell">seconds</span>
    </div>
    <div class="duel-row" role="row">
      <span class="duel-crit" role="rowheader">Memory <small>1.2 million flows</small></span>
      <span class="duel-a" role="cell"><span class="duel-bar" style="--w: 100%" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="num">1,350 MB</span></span>
      <span class="duel-b is-win" role="cell"
        ><span class="duel-bar" style="--w: 12.7%" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="num">171 MB</span></span
      >
    </div>
    <div class="duel-row" role="row">
      <span class="duel-crit" role="rowheader">Time per flow</span>
      <span class="duel-a is-win" role="cell">faster</span>
      <span class="duel-b" role="cell">slower</span>
    </div>
    <div class="duel-row" role="row">
      <span class="duel-crit" role="rowheader">Unknown traffic</span>
      <span class="duel-a" role="cell">no built-in check</span>
      <span class="duel-b is-win" role="cell">flagged, up to 0.92 AUROC</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    Two encrypted-traffic classifiers from our Computer Networks paper, tested on 184 applications (CESNET-TLS22). They are equally accurate, so the
    choice depends on the network: a backbone that needs every microsecond, or a gateway where new apps appear weekly and memory is tight. Highlighted
    cells mark the better side.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
